INT. DINNER THEATER - BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

A cramped corridor behind a painted flat. Costume racks, a mirror ringed with bulbs, a prop table covered in plastic daggers and one real turkey leg. Muffled laughter from the audience.

BARNABY CROWE (50s, leading man, powdered wig, gout slipper) strides in and flings his hat on the table. Behind him, DIANTHA FOSTER (30s, ingénue, corset half-laced) hurries, hem in her fist. Last in is STAGE MANAGER GWENDOLYN "GWEN" TAKAHASHI (40s, black clothes, headset, clipboard) who reads from her list as she walks.

BARNABY
(to everyone)
Who moved my snuffbox?

GWEN
It's on the mantle, Mr. Crowe.

BARNABY
There is no mantle!

GWEN
It's painted.

BARNABY
(muttering)
I will not play Hamlet opposite a painted mantle.

DIANTHA
Barnaby, it's "Much Ado."

BARNABY
Is it? I thought we'd done that.

GWEN
(to the room)
Five minutes, people.

DIANTHA
(low, to herself)
Breathe. Breathe. It's only two hundred people and a soup course.

BARNABY
Darling, you were magnificent in act one.

DIANTHA
I forgot my line.

BARNABY
Nobody noticed.

DIANTHA
You said "well-played" and then walked offstage.

BARNABY
It was a very good pause.

GWEN
Diantha, your cue is the bell. Mr. Crowe, yours is when she starts to cry.

BARNABY
She's crying?

DIANTHA
Not yet.

GWEN
Then enter on the bell.

A bell tolls, loud, from the stage. All three freeze, then bolt through the curtain in a flurry of lace and a clatter of turkey bones.
